                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94116/107: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Geneva</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Bucknell</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Geneva</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-09-30T00:00:00+01:00">September 30,
                        1938—midnight.</date><lb/>[Received September 30—7:10 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>260. My No. 254, September 29, 8 p.m. The Council today adopted the resolution
                    providing for investigation of Chinese charges of use of poison gas by Japanese
                    without change in text already reported. British stated that this was best
                    method of inquiry at least in first instance and China in accepting resolution
                    reserved the right to ask Council to take further action if present procedure
                    proved to be ineffective. Council session closed tonight.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bucknell</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
